/* AUTO-GENERATED by build.py from source/companies-extra/ - edit the source, not this file. */
LP.questionsBulk([{"q": "Amazon: Find products whose sales grew month-over-month for 3 straight months.", "a": "Aggregate to monthly sales per product, use `LAG()` over (partition by product order by month) to compare to the prior month, flag growth, then find 3 consecutive growth months with a gaps-and-islands grouping (`month_index - row_number()`). Mention indexing and that 'consecutive' needs a continuity check, not just 3 growth rows.", "topic": "SQL", "difficulty": "hard", "companies": ["Amazon"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Amazon: How do you guarantee a daily job is idempotent and self-healing?", "a": "Key the run on a **logical date**; write with **overwrite-by-partition** or **MERGE** so a rerun replaces (never duplicates). Make it incremental via a watermark. Add retries with backoff in the orchestrator, data-quality gates before publishing, and alerting. A failed/late run simply re-runs the affected partition. Ties to Ownership + Deliver Results.", "topic": "ETL", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["Amazon"], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Google: How would you evaluate a RAG system's quality?", "a": "Two axes: **retrieval** (are the right chunks returned? — recall@k, MRR) and **generation** (is the answer faithful to the retrieved context, and correct? — faithfulness/groundedness + answer accuracy). Build a labelled eval set, run automated evals (LLM-as-judge + exact/semantic match) plus human review, and track hallucination rate. Iterate on chunking first — it dominates quality.", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["Google"], "roles": ["GenAI Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Google: Explain how you'd debug a Spark job that's slow on one stage.", "a": "Open the Spark UI, find the slow stage, check for **skew** (one task much longer — uneven partition sizes), heavy **shuffle**/spill, or too few partitions. Fixes: broadcast the small side of joins, salt skewed keys, tune shuffle partitions / enable AQE, filter & select early, and avoid `collect()`. Verify the physical plan with `explain()`.", "topic": "Spark", "difficulty": "hard", "companies": ["Google"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Microsoft: Design an incremental ingestion framework reusable across 100+ source tables.", "a": "A **metadata-driven** framework: a control table per source (connection, watermark column, last-loaded value, load type). A generic job reads config, pulls `WHERE watermark > last_value` (or CDC), lands to Bronze, MERGEs to Silver idempotently, then advances the watermark. Add schema-evolution handling, DQ checks, retries, and logging. One engine, config per table — not 100 bespoke pipelines.", "topic": "System Design", "difficulty": "hard", "companies": ["Microsoft"], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Walmart: How do you handle schema changes from upstream source systems?", "a": "Land raw in **Bronze** so a schema change never breaks ingestion. Use **schema evolution** (Delta/Parquet) for additive changes; enforce compatibility via a **schema registry** or **data contracts** so producers can't silently break consumers. Alert on unexpected changes, version the schema, and quarantine bad records to a DLQ rather than failing the whole load.", "topic": "Concepts", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["Walmart"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "JPMC: Detect duplicate transactions that may be double-postings.", "a": "Group by the natural business key (account, amount, merchant, and a tight time window) and flag groups with count > 1, or use `ROW_NUMBER()` over that key ordered by timestamp and inspect rn > 1. Discuss tolerance windows and that true idempotency upstream (a unique txn id) prevents the problem at the source.", "topic": "SQL", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["JPMorgan Chase"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Apple: Write SQL for daily active users and the day-over-day change.", "a": "Count `DISTINCT user_id` per day, then `LAG()` to subtract the previous day:\n```sql\nWITH d AS (SELECT day, COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) dau FROM events GROUP BY day)\nSELECT day, dau, dau - LAG(dau) OVER (ORDER BY day) AS dod FROM d;\n```\nMention DISTINCT cost at scale and approximate counting (HLL) if needed.", "topic": "SQL", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["Apple"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Apple: How would you model data while respecting strict user privacy?", "a": "Separate PII into restricted tables with column/row-level access and encryption; use **surrogate keys** and tokenisation so analytics joins on pseudonymous ids; apply data minimisation, retention limits, and aggregation/differential-privacy where possible. Lineage + audit logs prove compliance. Privacy-by-design, not bolted on.", "topic": "Data Modeling", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["Apple"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Apple: A pipeline processing billions of device events is too slow. Approach?", "a": "Profile first. Likely wins: partition/cluster by event date, store columnar (Parquet), prune aggressively, fix skew (salting), broadcast small dims, right-size partitions (~128MB), avoid wide shuffles and `collect()`, and cache reused datasets. Consider incremental processing so you never reprocess all history.", "topic": "Spark", "difficulty": "hard", "companies": ["Apple"], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Apple: Tell me about a time you obsessed over quality/details.", "a": "Apple prizes craftsmanship. STAR: a case where you went beyond 'works' to 'correct and polished' — e.g. caught a subtle data-quality bug others missed, added rigorous tests, and the fix prevented a downstream incident. Show high standards and ownership of the end result.", "topic": "Behavioral", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["Apple"], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Apple: Batch vs streaming for analytics on device telemetry — how decide?", "a": "Decide by latency need and cost. Most analytics/reporting is fine as **batch** (hourly/daily) — simpler, cheaper. Use **streaming** only where seconds matter (real-time health/abuse detection). Often a hybrid: stream for alerts, batch for the authoritative aggregates (lambda-style).", "topic": "Concepts", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["Apple"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "IBM: What is data lineage and why does it matter in the enterprise?", "a": "Lineage traces where data came from and every transformation it passed through. It's essential for trust, debugging ('why is this number wrong?'), impact analysis (what breaks if I change this?), and compliance/audit. Tools: IBM tooling, OpenLineage, Unity Catalog, DataHub.", "topic": "ETL", "difficulty": "easy", "companies": ["IBM"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "IBM: Difference between a primary key, unique key, and foreign key.", "a": "**Primary key** uniquely identifies a row (one per table, not null). **Unique key** also enforces uniqueness but allows one null and you can have several. **Foreign key** references another table's PK to enforce referential integrity (links tables).", "topic": "SQL", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["IBM"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "IBM: What is hybrid cloud and why do enterprises use it?", "a": "**Hybrid cloud** mixes on-premise infrastructure with public/private cloud, with workloads and data spanning both. Enterprises use it for data-residency/compliance, gradual migration, keeping sensitive data on-prem while bursting compute to cloud, and avoiding lock-in. Adds networking, governance, and consistency challenges.", "topic": "Cloud", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["IBM"], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "IBM: What is data governance and a data catalog?", "a": "Governance = policies for data as an asset: lineage, access control, privacy/compliance, ownership, quality. A **data catalog** is the searchable inventory of datasets with metadata, owners, definitions, and lineage — so people can find and trust data. See [Data quality & governance](#/page/data-quality-governance).", "topic": "Concepts", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["IBM"], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "IBM: How would you build a governed enterprise RAG assistant?", "a": "Index approved documents into a vector DB with **metadata** (source, sensitivity, ACL). At query time, filter retrieval by the user's permissions, prompt the LLM to answer **only from retrieved context with citations**, log everything for audit, and add guardrails for PII/prompt-injection. Governance + lineage are first-class, not afterthoughts.", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["IBM"], "roles": ["AI Data Engineer", "GenAI Engineer"]}, {"q": "CGI: What are aggregate functions? Give examples.", "a": "Functions that summarise many rows into one value: `COUNT`, `SUM`, `AVG`, `MIN`, `MAX`. Used with `GROUP BY` to summarise per category. Remember `COUNT(*)` counts rows incl. nulls, while `COUNT(col)` skips nulls.", "topic": "SQL", "difficulty": "easy", "companies": ["CGI"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "CGI: Walk through how you'd migrate an on-prem warehouse to the cloud.", "a": "Assess sources & dependencies; pick target (Snowflake/BigQuery/Synapse); land raw to cloud storage; rebuild models (often ELT + dbt) in a medallion layout; migrate/validate data with reconciliation (row counts, checksums); run **parallel** old vs new until trust is established; cut over, then decommission. Plan governance, security, and cost throughout.", "topic": "ETL", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["CGI"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "CGI: How do you handle changing client requirements mid-project?", "a": "Clarify the underlying need, assess impact on scope/timeline, communicate trade-offs early and transparently, and prioritise with the client. Keep designs modular so change is cheaper. Show flexibility plus disciplined expectation-setting — core to consulting delivery.", "topic": "Behavioral", "difficulty": "easy", "companies": ["CGI"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "CGI: What is the difference between IaaS, PaaS, and SaaS?", "a": "**IaaS** = rent raw infrastructure (VMs, storage) — you manage the rest (EC2). **PaaS** = managed platform to run apps without managing servers (App Engine, managed DBs). **SaaS** = ready software you just use (Gmail, Salesforce). Less control but less ops as you go up the stack.", "topic": "Cloud", "difficulty": "easy", "companies": ["CGI"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Oracle: How do you optimise a slow query? What does the optimizer use?", "a": "Read the **execution plan** (`EXPLAIN PLAN`/`AUTOTRACE`): look for full table scans vs index access, bad join order, and cardinality misestimates. Ensure **statistics** are fresh (the cost-based optimizer relies on them), add indexes on filter/join columns, avoid functions on indexed columns, and consider partitioning. Bind variables help reuse plans.", "topic": "SQL", "difficulty": "hard", "companies": ["Oracle"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Oracle: What is the difference between a view and a materialized view?", "a": "A **view** is a stored query — it runs every time you select from it (always fresh, no storage). A **materialized view** stores the *result* physically and must be refreshed (fast reads, possibly stale). Use MVs to precompute expensive aggregations.", "topic": "SQL", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["Oracle"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Oracle: Explain table partitioning and its benefits.", "a": "Splitting a large table into segments by a key (range by date, list, hash). Benefits: **partition pruning** (scan only relevant partitions), faster maintenance (drop a partition vs delete rows), parallelism, and easier archival. The single biggest lever for large-table performance.", "topic": "Data Modeling", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["Oracle"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Oracle: What do ACID isolation levels protect against?", "a": "They trade safety vs concurrency and prevent anomalies: **dirty reads** (reading uncommitted data), **non-repeatable reads** (a row changes between reads), and **phantom reads** (new rows appear). Levels from Read Uncommitted → Read Committed → Repeatable Read → Serializable (safest, slowest). See [ACID](#/page/acid-transactions).", "topic": "Concepts", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["Oracle"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "SAP: Star schema vs snowflake — when would you normalize a dimension?", "a": "Default to **star** (flat dims, fewer joins, fast). Snowflake (normalize a dimension into sub-tables) only when a dimension is huge, shared across facts, or has rapidly-changing reference data where storage/consistency outweighs the extra joins.", "topic": "Data Modeling", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["SAP"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "SAP: How do you handle delta (incremental) loads from a source system?", "a": "Track a watermark (timestamp/changed-flag) or use CDC to capture only new/changed records since the last run, then MERGE/upsert into the target idempotently and advance the watermark. Handle deletes explicitly (soft-delete flags or CDC delete events).", "topic": "ETL", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["SAP"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "SAP: What is an index and what are its trade-offs?", "a": "A sorted structure (usually B-tree) that speeds lookups/joins/sorts by avoiding full scans — at the cost of extra storage and slower writes (every insert/update maintains the index). Index selectively on high-selectivity filter/join columns.", "topic": "SQL", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["SAP"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Salesforce: Design a pipeline to unify customer data from many sources (a 'customer 360').", "a": "Ingest each source to Bronze; standardise & clean to Silver; resolve identity with **entity resolution** (deterministic keys + fuzzy matching) into a unified customer key; build a Gold customer-360 mart (often with SCD2 for changing attributes). Add data contracts per source, DQ checks, and lineage. Serve to BI, marketing, and ML/feature store.", "topic": "System Design", "difficulty": "hard", "companies": ["Salesforce"], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Salesforce: De-duplicate customer records that aren't exact matches.", "a": "Exact dupes: group by a business key and keep one via `ROW_NUMBER()`. Fuzzy dupes: normalise fields (lowercase, trim, standardise phone/email), block on a coarse key, then score similarity (e.g. edit distance) and merge above a threshold — i.e. **entity resolution**, not just `GROUP BY`.", "topic": "SQL", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["Salesforce"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Salesforce: Tell me about working with cross-functional stakeholders.", "a": "STAR: show you translated business needs into data requirements, set a clear data contract/SLA, communicated trade-offs in plain language, and delivered something stakeholders trusted and used. Emphasise collaboration and customer focus (a core Salesforce value).", "topic": "Behavioral", "difficulty": "easy", "companies": ["Salesforce"], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Nvidia: How do you serve a large LLM efficiently for high throughput?", "a": "Use **GPU inference** with **continuous/iteration batching** (e.g. vLLM) to keep the GPU busy across requests, **quantization** (int8/4-bit) to cut memory/latency, KV-cache management, tensor/pipeline parallelism for big models, and autoscaling. Measure tokens/sec and p99 latency; balance batch size vs latency.", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "hard", "companies": ["Nvidia"], "roles": ["LLM Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Nvidia: How would you prepare a massive dataset for distributed model training?", "a": "Clean and shard data into balanced, shuffled files (Parquet/WebDataset); ensure even partitioning to avoid stragglers; pre-tokenise/precompute features to keep GPUs fed; use a feature/data pipeline that streams without bottlenecking on I/O; and validate reproducibility (versioned datasets). I/O, not compute, is often the bottleneck.", "topic": "Spark", "difficulty": "hard", "companies": ["Nvidia"], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Nvidia: What is distributed training (data vs model parallelism)?", "a": "**Data parallelism**: replicate the model on each GPU, split the *data*, sync gradients (all-reduce). **Model/tensor parallelism**: split the *model* itself across GPUs when it's too big for one. **Pipeline parallelism**: split by layers across stages. Large LLMs combine all three. Frameworks: Ray, DeepSpeed, Megatron.", "topic": "Concepts", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["Nvidia"], "roles": ["LLM Engineer"]}, {"q": "LinkedIn: Design the pipeline behind 'People You May Know' style features.", "a": "Ingest interaction/graph events via **Kafka** (LinkedIn built Kafka); land raw; build graph features (mutual connections, co-occurrence) in Spark; serve candidate features from a low-latency store + feature store for the model; precompute heavy graph traversals offline. Discuss freshness vs cost, feature parity train/serve, and A/B testing.", "topic": "System Design", "difficulty": "hard", "companies": ["LinkedIn"], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "LinkedIn: Explain Kafka offsets and consumer groups.", "a": "Each partition is an ordered log; an **offset** marks how far a consumer has read (committed offsets let it resume after a crash). A **consumer group** splits a topic's partitions among its members so they scale out — each partition is consumed by exactly one member of the group. Different groups read independently.", "topic": "Streaming", "difficulty": "hard", "companies": ["LinkedIn"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "LinkedIn: Lambda vs Kappa architecture — which and why?", "a": "**Lambda** runs batch + speed layers in parallel (accurate history + fast fresh data) but duplicates logic. **Kappa** is stream-only and reprocesses by replaying the log — one codebase. Kappa fits when a strong streaming platform (Kafka + a stream processor) can handle reprocessing; lambda when batch correctness/tools are essential.", "topic": "Concepts", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["LinkedIn"], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Visa: Design a low-latency fraud-scoring pipeline for card transactions.", "a": "Transactions → **Kafka** → stream processor (Flink) computing velocity/geo/amount features against recent history in a fast store → model scores in milliseconds → allow/deny + alert; raw + features also land in the lakehouse for retraining/audit. Emphasise **exactly-once** for money, low p99 latency, feature parity, drift monitoring, and strong security/governance.", "topic": "System Design", "difficulty": "hard", "companies": ["Visa"], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Visa: Why is exactly-once processing critical in payments, and how is it achieved?", "a": "Double-processing = double-charging or lost funds. Achieve effective exactly-once with durable, replayable ingestion (Kafka + offsets), **idempotent** writes (MERGE on a unique txn id), checkpointing in the stream processor, and transactional sinks (Delta/Iceberg). At-least-once delivery + idempotency = exactly-once in practice.", "topic": "Concepts", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["Visa"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Visa: How do you secure sensitive payment data in a pipeline?", "a": "Encrypt at rest and in transit, tokenise/mask PAN and PII, enforce least-privilege access (row/column-level), keep immutable audit logs, segregate sensitive zones, and apply retention/compliance (PCI-DSS). Lineage proves where regulated data flows.", "topic": "Concepts", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["Visa"], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Citi: Compute a 30-day rolling average of transaction volume per account.", "a": "```sql\nSELECT account_id, day, AVG(volume) OVER (\n  PARTITION BY account_id ORDER BY day\n  ROWS BETWEEN 29 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS rolling_30d\nFROM daily_volume;\n```\nNote: this is a row-based window (assumes one row per day) — use a range window or a calendar table if days can be missing.", "topic": "SQL", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["Citi"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Citi: How do you ensure data quality and auditability for regulatory reporting?", "a": "Quality-as-code: schema + business-rule tests with severity (fail/warn/info), reconciliation of counts/totals vs source, freshness SLAs, and failures logged to an audit table. For audit: full lineage, immutable logs, versioned data, and access controls. See [Data quality & governance](#/page/data-quality-governance).", "topic": "Concepts", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["Citi"], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Citi: Describe a time you caught a data error before it reached production/regulators.", "a": "STAR with rigour: a DQ check or reconciliation you built that flagged an anomaly (e.g. a totals mismatch), how you root-caused it, fixed the pipeline, and added a guard to prevent recurrence. Show carefulness and accountability — essential in regulated finance.", "topic": "Behavioral", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["Citi"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "ZS: Rank customers within each region by revenue and return the top 3 per region.", "a": "```sql\nWITH r AS (SELECT region, customer, revenue,\n  ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY region ORDER BY revenue DESC) rn\n  FROM sales)\nSELECT * FROM r WHERE rn <= 3;\n```\nUse `RANK()`/`DENSE_RANK()` instead if ties should share a position.", "topic": "SQL", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["ZS Associates"], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "Data Scientist"]}, {"q": "ZS: How would you build a reusable data pipeline for many similar client datasets?", "a": "Make it **config/metadata-driven**: parameterise sources, schema mappings, and transformations so one engine serves many clients. Standardise to a common model, add per-dataset DQ rules, and keep raw landing for reprocessing. Avoids copy-pasting bespoke pipelines per client.", "topic": "ETL", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["ZS Associates"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "ZS: How do you turn an ambiguous business question into a data solution?", "a": "Clarify the decision the client wants to make, define the metric precisely, identify the data needed and its quality, propose an approach with assumptions stated, and validate findings before presenting. Structured problem-solving + clear communication is the consulting core.", "topic": "Behavioral", "difficulty": "easy", "companies": ["ZS Associates"], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "Data Scientist"]}, {"q": "LTIMindtree: What is the difference between WHERE and HAVING (with an example)?", "a": "`WHERE` filters rows before grouping; `HAVING` filters groups after aggregation.\n```sql\nSELECT dept, COUNT(*) c FROM emp\nWHERE active = 1            -- per-row\nGROUP BY dept HAVING COUNT(*) > 10;  -- per-group\n```", "topic": "SQL", "difficulty": "easy", "companies": ["LTIMindtree"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "LTIMindtree: What are narrow vs wide transformations in Spark?", "a": "**Narrow** transformations (map, filter) need data only from one partition — no shuffle, fast. **Wide** transformations (groupBy, join, distinct) need data from multiple partitions — they trigger a **shuffle** across the network, which is expensive. Minimising wide transformations/shuffles is key to performance.", "topic": "Spark", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": ["LTIMindtree"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "LTIMindtree: What is the difference between a data lake and a data warehouse?", "a": "A **lake** stores raw data of any type cheaply (schema-on-read, flexible); a **warehouse** stores structured, modelled data for fast governed SQL (schema-on-write). A **lakehouse** combines both via a table layer (Delta/Iceberg). See [Warehouses & lakehouse](#/page/warehouse-basics).", "topic": "ETL", "difficulty": "easy", "companies": ["LTIMindtree"], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}]);
